WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.250
Film Courage: How do you define story?

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:05.030
How do you teach it to clients and students?

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:06.030
&nbsp;

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:09.629
Glenn Gers, Screenwriter: I think the basic
idea of a story is that you’re going to

00:00:09.629 --> 00:00:12.480
be following a character or characters.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.830
It’s entirely possible to tell the story
of multiple characters.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:18.349
You don’t only have to tell one person’s
story.

00:00:18.349 --> 00:00:23.720
It’s nice, you can do it, but it is also
possible to tell a story with 11 main characters.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.460
It takes a lot of work.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:30.460
You have to follow each main character and
follow their story all the way through the

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:36.950
narrative but in fact I believe any story
is really about how a character trying to

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:41.940
accomplish something runs into other people
who will help or harm their intention.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:48.789
It’s sort of like Isaac Newton’s theory
of billiard balls that once they get set in

00:00:48.789 --> 00:00:54.420
motion they will role in that same direction
because they are being pushed by a physical

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.440
force like a character is also doing that.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.550
They are trying to get somewhere and they
are rolling in a certain direction until either

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:05.939
they hit something or something comes in and
knocks into them then that changes their direction

00:01:05.939 --> 00:01:09.290
but they are still trying to get to that one
place.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.710
The physics kind of falls apart there because
billiard balls are not trying to get anywhere

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:16.700
but the idea that things are moving until
they run into something else which is also

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:20.270
moving is sort of how characters work.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:23.259
Every character thinks they’re the main
character.

00:01:23.259 --> 00:01:29.979
If you have your hero walk into a hotel and
try to get a hotel room that hotel clerk they

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:35.409
think they’re the center of the movie because
they are the center of their story and this

00:01:35.409 --> 00:01:40.720
person coming up to them is with getting in
their way or trying to charm them or whatever

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:46.009
it is that they’re doing, they are trying
to do something and that interchange is the

00:01:46.009 --> 00:01:48.119
basic building blocks of story.

00:01:48.119 --> 00:01:53.860
That’s what you cal dramatic action trying
to accomplish something which mostly involves

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.759
interacting with other people.

00:01:55.759 --> 00:01:59.890
Sometimes the action can be I have to take
this suitcase and put in on a train, whatever.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:05.790
But if will still be I have a thing I am trying
to accomplish and that’s what story is.

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:07.950
I have a thing I am trying to accomplish.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:12.710
I am a character and I need to get this thing
and it will be a more interesting story if

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:14.450
there is something in the way.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:18.730
If I am a character and I am trying to put
a suitcase on a train and I go and put it

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:22.240
on the train and nothing happens it’s a
very short, boring story.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:27.370
If they are carrying the suitcase and another
person comes and they steal the suitcase,

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:32.680
it’s more of an interesting story and it’s
a more interesting story if that person’s

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.170
got something important in the suitcase.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:36.180
If it’s just a suitcase and they say Oh?

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:42.050
Something stole it, I’ll go get another
one - not that strong&nbsp; a story.&nbsp;

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:47.710
Every story is about a character trying to
accomplish something and having an obstacle

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:55.300
and what they do and the action they take
in the face of obstacles is your story.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.690
The obstacles don’t have to be external.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.370
The obstacles can be I am really afraid of
the train station.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:05.360
I need to get this suitcase to my uncle and
he’s going to be at the other end of the

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:11.680
train so I’ve got to get the suitcase onto
the train but I’m terrified of loud noises,

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.230
that’s a story.

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:16.380
The only obstacle is in there but it’s real.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:22.860
So every character is facing obstacles, the
obstacles don’t have to be physical, they

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.900
don’t have to be another character.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.350
They just have to be something that is getting
in the way of trying to accomplish something

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:29.730
important to them.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:35.100
Film Courage: Can you explain how questions
and choices inform the writing process?

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:38.300
Glenn: Writing is a process of questions.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:44.040
There are a couple of things that I wish I
could get tattooed inside peoples’ eyelids

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:49.560
if they knew - think in scenes and writing
is a process of questions.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:54.810
It’s not a thing you need to fill out, it’s
not a form you have to fit into.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:04:01.790
Writing is a question of having something…it
could just be I want to write a Western or

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:08.280
I want to talk about how love hurts or I want
to talk about how love saved my life.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.310
Whatever it is you start with then you start
to ask questions.

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:14.260
How am I going to tell this story?

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:17.680
Am I going to tell it through a character
who gets it or who doesn’t get it.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:19.450
Every thing is going to be a choice.

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:25.150
Every question that you ask if you write down
that question how am I going to tell this

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:26.150
story?

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:27.570
Who is the main character?

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:31.530
Everything is a question and those questions
are: Who is it about?

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:33.280
What do they want?

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.220
Why can’t they get it?

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.730
What do they do about that?

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:38.900
And how does it end?

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:45.020
I guess I skipped one…oh…why doesn’t
that work, right.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:46.110
Who is it about?

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:47.330
What do they want?

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:48.860
Why can’t they get it?

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:50.160
What do they do about that?

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:51.780
Why doesn’t that work?

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:52.780
How does it end?

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:55.610
I did a whole video on this called the 6 Essential
Questions [on Glenn’s Youtube channel Writing

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:56.610
For Screens].

00:04:56.610 --> 00:05:02.440
I explained it better there but those 6 questions
practically will help you write anything.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:06.240
They will help you write a movie, they will
help you write a video game, they will help

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:13.071
you write a series because that’s the essential
thing you get of how am I going to turn in

00:05:13.071 --> 00:05:16.220
whatever feeling or idea I have into a story?

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:21.150
Is a person who is trying to do something
to get something and there is something in

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:26.730
the way and then eventually something will
end it either the end will be I don’t get

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:33.220
it or I do get it and whatever they do that
they had never tried before is how it ends

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:35.640
because if they tried before it would have
ended before.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.850
So who is it about?

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:38.850
What do they want?

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:40.220
What do they do?

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:41.560
Why doesn’t that work?

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:42.930
What do they finally do?

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:44.380
What is the end?

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:49.800
That is storytelling and it can work for three
acts or twenty-seven acts or one act.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:51.270
It always works.

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:55.730
It’s my go to set of questions.

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:58.240
And then keep asking questions.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:05:59.240
Who is it about?

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:01.240
It’s about a plumber?

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:02.240
Where is he from?

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:04.650
Just ask - who, what, why, where?

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:06.540
Keep asking why.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.730
Why is he afraid of heights?

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:13.190
Why does he love this particular person?

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:16.960
Every time that you ask a question you get
a specific answer and you are moving closer

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:18.240
to writing a scene.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.910
Film Courage: What is the easiest way for
someone to figure out an enjoyable writing

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:25.240
process for themselves?

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:31.690
Glenn: The first important thing about this
process is recognizing that it’s yours.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:33.990
There is not a right or wrong way.

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:38.530
Some people write at night, some people write
in the day, some people write in short bursts,

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:41.340
some people write in long extended bursts.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:45.180
There is no particular better or worse process.

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:50.430
An important thing about a process is that
it’s something that you can do relatively

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:56.100
easily for whatever reason it works for you
and that means you have to spend time paying

00:06:56.100 --> 00:07:00.250
attention to yourself, trying different things,
seeing which ones work and which ones don’t

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:02.180
work and being really honest.

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:10.810
I personally (I keep doing that, sorry) I
turn out to write well in very short bursts.

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:16.810
I thought I should do more because when writing
in short bursts it’s like Holy Crap!

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:20.890
If I can keep doing this for eight hours I’d
be a miracle!

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:27.820
But I can’t and I would sit there and grind
after the burst was running down…grind and

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:33.990
not only would that be unproductive and disheartening
but I would then start to undo my good work

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:39.700
because when you start to grind in a bad way,
you start to doubt, you start to feel bad

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:44.330
and say Well I must be feeling bad because
this work is bad which is not necessarily

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:45.330
the case.

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:47.380
It could be that your process is bad.

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:50.370
And so what I would do is I would write something
that was really good and then I would grind

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:55.720
until I was unhappy and then I would say Oh
that’s sucks and then I would write something

00:07:55.720 --> 00:08:00.760
over it and destroy my good work and it took
me a long time to pay attention to the fact

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:07.060
that I do really well in short bursts and
if I stop when I feel myself starting to lose

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:13.590
it and take a break, take a walk, do exercises
(whatever) I can then come back and do another

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:15.290
short burst.

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:20.260
Getting myself to sit down again is rough
but that is the thing I had to teach myself

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:23.090
in paying attention to what worked.

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:27.260
I’ve gotten much more productive since I’ve
learned my process.

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:29.380
It’s not for everyone.

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:34.690
Everyone will have a process that is dependent
on their own inner mechanisms and on their

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:36.270
own reality.

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:39.390
Some people only have free time on weekends.

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:45.110
Some people only get a little free time in
the evening and so they have to find a way

00:08:45.110 --> 00:08:49.640
to work at the time that they are allowed
by their life.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:54.680
You have to pay attention to reality and pay
attention to your own inner working and the

00:08:54.680 --> 00:09:00.190
best to find out is to do it and see how it
goes.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:04.900
It’s always better to try and do some work
and see how it goes.

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:09.910
You will never get to the place where you
absolutely know that you can write and then

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:10.910
you start writing.

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:15.140
There is always the question of let me try
this and see what comes out.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:17.920
That’s the best test of a process.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:24.040
Film Courage: And working these temp jobs
for many other reasons aside from income and

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:27.740
the study of people helped you realize that
was your preferred style?

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:32.320
Glenn: Forced me because I was stubborn and
I would just keep doing the wrong thing over

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:36.840
and over again until circumstances forced
me to work in short bursts and then I was

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:39.750
like Wow, that actually is better!

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:47.230
Trying to be open to your process, trying
to pay attention to what actually makes the

00:09:47.230 --> 00:09:49.800
work good and feels good.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:52.310
Feeling good is underrated.

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:55.029
You have to learn to write when you don’t
feel good.

00:09:55.029 --> 00:10:01.720
For me having a process where I can say I
have five basic documents that I open like

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:08.540
an outline, a place where I write notes, and
the text itself and a kind of overview (I’m

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:11.331
going to be doing a video on this soon - Writing
For Screens Youtube channel), that’s my

00:10:11.331 --> 00:10:13.020
personal set up.

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:18.921
Everyone has their own but what you need to
do is so when you open that notebook you are

00:10:18.921 --> 00:10:22.450
not thinking about anything but the contents
of the work.

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:24.230
You know Ah, this is the outline.

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:27.610
The outline is going to be broken into scenes
and the scene lines are going to look like

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:32.110
this and I don’t have to think about that,
I have to think about what is in the scene.

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:36.890
That is the best way to find a process is
to get something that works for you.

00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:39.640
Some people will do it on their phone.

00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:46.690
I would advise doing it in some way that is
written down in some form, digitally, on paper,

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:47.860
on notecards.

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:52.720
It doesn’t matter, what matters is you get
it out of your head and into the world in

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:56.690
some way that you can set it aside and come
back to it, set it aside and come back to

00:10:56.690 --> 00:11:00.830
it because with almost anything you are going
to have to, you can’t do it all at once.

00:11:00.830 --> 00:11:06.520
Film Courage: When did you have that aha moment
that I don’t have writer’s block and my

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:14.540
anxiety was that wasn’t my style to sit
at my desk in my parents’ basement or wherever

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:20.320
and with the perfect music and the iced tea,
that actually wasn’t working for me.

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:25.620
Glenn: It was unfortunately not an aha moment,
it was an aha couple of years.

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:33.630
And I think that is sort of a misleading thing
that our culture has developed from movies

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:38.180
that there is a decisive moment and after
it you are always changed.

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:39.910
In story that is a very important.

00:11:39.910 --> 00:11:44.760
You need decisive moments when people are
changed but it truth it’s more like a series

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:49.680
and I have a couple seasons where I get a
little bit of it and then I fall back into

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:54.170
my own ways and then I have some rewards and
then I do something new.

00:11:54.170 --> 00:12:01.740
It’s a process in which you try to do two
steps forward, one step back not the other

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:03.620
way around because you’re trying to head
forward.

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:08.089
You do a little forward, you fall back, a
little forward, you fall back, and then the

00:12:08.089 --> 00:12:13.070
main way it always is the doing of it.

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:18.561
Just throw something on the paper even if
it’s just (and I done this) I have a scene

00:12:18.561 --> 00:12:24.560
where I say this is the scene where he comes
home and is miserable and I just took it out

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:30.260
of the outline and wrote it into the script
because next time I look at it I am now familiar

00:12:30.260 --> 00:12:31.260
with this process.

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:35.970
I now say I created a scene, the scene is
a description, this is where he comes home

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:37.320
and is miserable.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:40.020
Okay, how do we see that he is miserable?

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:41.630
Good question.

00:12:41.630 --> 00:12:47.100
He seems miserable because he takes his dinner
out of the refrigerator and throws it on the

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:48.560
wall.

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:53.580
Each thing that you get when you ask a question
to me it’s always a process of questions

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:56.140
and a process of writing down the answer.

00:12:56.140 --> 00:13:00.480
Now I have a scene where he comes in, opens
his fridge, throws the dinner against the

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:01.480
wall.

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:02.560
Okay, we know he’s miserable.

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:07.060
Now I can add a couple of lines and move onto
the next scene.

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:12.910
Everything you can do to put a little something
down to create a bit of something for your

00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:18.730
creativity to hang onto that is (for me) the
essence of the writing process.

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:27.590
Film Courage: So if this were a movie, it
would show Glenn as a temp and then “Alright,

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:28.600
Charlie…have a good weekend!”

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:34.230
And then the music plays and you have a half
hour and this is it, here I am writing it.

00:13:34.230 --> 00:13:39.180
Glenn: I actually think what we would do is
we would show…we would cut to the imaginary

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:44.790
scene where we’d play it out and so we’d
get to catch the thrill of the creation by

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:49.450
seeing the creative magic of this character…sorry
I’m writing now.

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:52.140
Film Courage: Now I love it.

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:53.140
So Glenn as the writer.

00:13:53.140 --> 00:13:57.530
Glenn: I’m sitting there writing the movie
and we cut to or dissolve to…by the way…that’s

00:13:57.530 --> 00:13:58.570
another thing.

00:13:58.570 --> 00:14:03.330
Most of the time a) don’t write ‘CUT TO.’
William Goldman started it, it’s a delightful

00:14:03.330 --> 00:14:06.519
thing in his scripts, obviously they are going
to cut to.

00:14:06.519 --> 00:14:07.860
How else are they going to get there.

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:11.850
You are wasting page space, don’t say CUT
TO.

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:17.950
And most of the time don’t say DISSOLVE
TO or FADE OUT because that’s their decision

00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:23.230
unless it’s really important that you dissolve,
just write the next scene and they will figure

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:25.130
out how to get there.

00:14:25.130 --> 00:14:31.390
Anyway, get to Glenn writing and then show
that scene where the character is running

00:14:31.390 --> 00:14:36.120
to the phone booth in the rain and it’s
a great scene and people will get so excited

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:40.380
as my creativity as a character that they
will want to know what happens next.

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:40.720
Question For The Viewers: What are your best
writing questions?

